Take the old left-hand side as it is in update.formula() when the new
formula has none, so the formula target stops running out of memory.

updateform() in src/library/stats/src/model.c gave a new formula without
a lhs the old lhs object itself, then ran ExpandDots() over it with that
same object as the replacement: each "." became a duplicate() of the
tree being modified, which already held the earlier copies.  The lhs
grew exponentially in its number of dots, and the caller's formula was
modified in place.  An input with 335 dots in the lhs exhausted memory
(oss-fuzz testcase 6137429483126784).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #544 (no Bugzilla report yet).
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

--- a/src/library/stats/src/model.c
+++ b/src/library/stats/src/model.c
@@ -934,17 +934,17 @@ SEXP updateform(SEXP old, SEXP new)
     if (length(old) == 3) {
 	SEXP lhs = CADR(old);
 	SEXP rhs = CADDR(old);
-	/* We now check that new formula has a valid lhs.
-	   If it doesn't, we add one and set it to the rhs of the old
-	   formula. */
+	/* Substitute the old sides for any "." templates in the new
+	   formula.  We must parenthesize the rhs or we might upset arity
+	   and precedence.  A new formula without a lhs takes the old lhs
+	   as it is: that lhs is not part of the template, and expanding
+	   its own dots with itself, while it is being modified, would
+	   grow it exponentially and change 'old'. */
 	if (length(_new) == 2)
 	    SETCDR(_new, CONS(lhs, CDR(_new)));
-	/* Now we check the left and right sides of the new formula
-	   and substitute the correct value for any "." templates.
-	   We must parenthesize the rhs or we might upset arity and
-	   precedence. */
+	else
+	    SETCADR(_new, ExpandDots(CADR(_new), lhs));
 	PROTECT(rhs);
-	SETCADR(_new, ExpandDots(CADR(_new), lhs));
 	SETCADDR(_new, ExpandDots(CADDR(_new), rhs));
 	UNPROTECT(1);
     }
